From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 01:03:41 -0300
Subject: drm/nouveau/dispnv50: support Broadcast RGB and content type on HDMI

Desktop compositors offer a display's output range and content type
through the generic "Broadcast RGB" and "content type" connector
properties, which nouveau didn't attach: its heads always sent full
range RGB with a full-range AVI InfoFrame.

On Turing and newer (C57D and CA7D heads), limited range is what the
hardware has for it, programmed as OpenRM does in its PROCAMP setup:
PROCAMP's dynamic range set to CEA, OCSC1 compressing RGB to 16-235
(OpenRM's RGBToLimitedRangeRGB coefficients) and the output clamped to
16-235. The AVI InfoFrame carries the range the head sends and the
content type. "Broadcast RGB" is attached on HDMI from Turing on, where
it is implemented; "Automatic" keeps nouveau's full range, as before.
A change to either property goes out with a modeset.

The clamp and OCSC1 methods are added to the C57D and CA7D class
headers as NVIDIA's open class headers define them.

Compile-tested against 7.3.0-rc1; not yet run on hardware.

Assisted-by: Claude-Opus-5.5
(cherry picked from commit 102ea42fefbcc29a8e2376b2b7008a34bf439818)
